sz.py	Argument check plus FileNotFoundError, PermissionError, and UnicodeDecodeError with clear msgs; guard before max; findall plus int conversion is clean… nice. Comments read tight. Any thoughts on finding strings of integers seperated by comma's, like on line 2896 of the input file: "almost 559,000 of your miles"?
uf.py	find_greatest_integer path with line-by-line scan is a good instinct for bigger files; structure reads like real homework. One minus: add PermissionError and UnicodeDecodeError on open the way several others in class did so every boring failure prints something helpful.
nx.py	Nice helper and specific IndexError/FileNotFoundError/PermissionError flow; match objects handled right. Consider with open(...) and empty-set guard before max() so demo day does not blow up on a weird file with no digits.
hj.py	Super short readable path argv -> read -> findall -> loop max; regex choice is fine. Replace bare except on open with named errors; handle "no numbers" before you print biggest so nobody thinks the book contained real zeros when it did not.
55.py	Line walk with findall and append then max matches how a lot of us attacked it. Swap bare except for explicit file errors; guard empty list before max(); rename filter so it does not fight the builtin named filter… small polish win.
ra.py	You split tokens then pull digits… more loops than scan-the-whole-string but it can work. Critical fix: sys.exit after each open error branch so txt_file.read never runs on a broken handle; add empty guard before max(). Any thoughts on comma grouped integers splitting into smaller chunks with plain r'\d+'?
yy.py	You actually reached for commas and signs in regex which stands out compared to vanilla findall digits only; helper layout is intentional. Trim broad except blocks, hide the debug dump of twenty ints unless the assignment calls for it, and use with open for closing.
